# 0001. Show a wrenched bike as disabled in the feed

Status: proposed. Date: 2026-11-13. Spec: `work/spec.md` AC-1.

## Context and Problem Statement

When a rider sends `POST /wrench`, the bike must stop looking rentable in the public GBFS feed
at once (AC-1, SLA-12: "accurate and available to the public at all times", 60 second limit).
Today a wrenched bike still shows as rentable. How should `vehicle_status.json` show a bike
that has an open wrench?

## Considered Options

1. Keep the bike in `vehicle_status.json` with `is_disabled: true` until it is fixed.
2. Drop the bike from `vehicle_status.json` until it is fixed.

## Decision Outcome

Chosen option: 1, "keep the bike in the feed with `is_disabled: true`", because it is what
GBFS v3 defines the field for. The reference says:

```text
This field is used to indicate vehicles that are in the field but not available for rental
due to a mechanical issue or low battery etc. Publishing this data may prevent users from
attempting to rent vehicles that are disabled and not available for rental.
```

A wrenched bike is still in the field and has a mechanical
issue, so it fits exactly. It also keeps the vehicle feed in line with `station_status.json`,
where AC-4 needs `num_vehicles_disabled` to rise by one. Dropping the bike would leave the
station counting a disabled bike that the vehicle feed no longer lists.

## Consequences

- Good, because riders and third-party apps can see the bike is there but cannot be rented,
  which is the warning the GBFS reference describes.
- Good, because the vehicle and station feeds agree (AC-3, AC-4), and the map follows (AC-5).
- Good, because it reuses the existing `bikes.is_disabled` column. No new state is needed.
- Bad, because feed users now see more disabled bikes, so they need notice (spec C-4).
- Bad, because the bike stays disabled until a job can be closed, and no route does that yet
  (spec C-3). This adds to the SLA-9 risk in C-2. Option 2 has the same problem.
# The service levels in Kestrel's contract with Brannock City Transport.
# The shape follows the service levels in Citi Bike's monthly report to New York City:
# https://mot-marketing-whitelabel-prod.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/nyc/April-2026-Citi-Bike-Monthly-Report.pdf
# Nobody edits this file except the city. Claude Code must never change it.
contract: Brannock City Transport and Kestrel Bike Share
service_levels:
  - id: SLA-4
    name: Bike defects
    rule: A rider pressing the wrench button is notice of a defect.
    clock_starts: when the rider reports the defect (the wrench)
    wrenched_limit_hours: 48
    not_wrenched_limit_hours: 96
    target_percent: 95
    credit_per_late_bike_gbp: 50
  - id: SLA-9
    name: Bike availability
    rule: The share of the fleet that riders can rent, across every district.
    minimum_percent: 96
  - id: SLA-11
    name: Station outage
    rule: No single station outage lasts longer than this.
    limit_hours: 4
  - id: SLA-12
    name: Public feed accuracy
    rule: Real-time data in the public feed is accurate and available to the public at all times.
    feed_max_age_seconds: 60
